<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8"/>
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"/>
    <base href="/"/>
    <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css?family=Roboto:300,400,500,700&display=swap" rel="stylesheet"/>
    <link href=@Assets["_content/MudBlazor/MudBlazor.min.css"] rel="stylesheet"/>
    <ImportMap/>
@*
    Prerendering is deliberately off, here and on Routes below.

    It used to be impossible: the JWT lived in the browser's localStorage, which the server
    cannot read, and every render resolved the authentication state through a JS interop call
    that throws when there is no browser. ADR 0009 removed that obstacle — the identity is now
    a cookie this host reads, so it does know the caller at render time.

    What remains is the reason that was always the stronger one. The pages are built on
    interactive MudBlazor controls, which a prerendered pass renders inert: a login form the
    user can see and type into but that silently drops the click until WebAssembly boots. The
    pages that load data do so in OnInitializedAsync, which prerendering runs twice — once
    server-side, for a first paint that is replaced moments later.

    A second reason used to sit beside it — the application was entirely behind authentication,
    so there was no SEO to gain. ADR 0062 makes that half false: /catalog and /catalog/{id}
    are public, and a public catalog is exactly the kind of page a crawler is meant to read.
    The half that decided is untouched, and it is the one above.

    So the question is genuinely open now, and narrower than it was: prerendering these two
    routes and no others. It is a decision about perceived performance and discoverability, to
    be made against a measurement. It is not made here.
*@
    <HeadOutlet @rendermode="new InteractiveWebAssemblyRenderMode(prerender: false)"/>
</head>

<body>
<Routes @rendermode="new InteractiveWebAssemblyRenderMode(prerender: false)"/>
<script src="_framework/blazor.web.js"></script>
<script src=@Assets["_content/MudBlazor/MudBlazor.min.js"]></script>
</body>

</html>
